          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca\",\"url\":\"https://react.dev/blog/2024/12/05/react-19\",\"title\":\"React v19 – React\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:19.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 0-24]\\nBLOG\\nCopy page\\nReact v19\\n\\n[characters 26-61]\\nDecember 05, 2024 by The React Team\\n\\n[characters 63-92]\\nNote\\nReact 19 is now stable! \\n\\n[characters 94-172]\\nAdditions since this post was originally shared with the React 19 RC in April:\\n\\n[characters 174-290]\\nPre-warming for suspended trees: see Improvements to Suspense.\\nReact DOM static APIs: see New React DOM Static APIs.\\n\\n[characters 292-367]\\nThe date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\\n\\n[characters 369-403]\\nReact v19 is now available on npm!\\n\\n[characters 405-605]\\nIn our React 19 Upgrade Guide, we shared step-by-step instructions for upgrading your app to React 19. In this post, we’ll give an overview of the new features in React 19, and how you can adopt them.\\n\\n[characters 607-669]\\nWhat’s new in React 19\\nImprovements in React 19\\nHow to upgrade\\n\\n[characters 671-725]\\nFor a list of breaking changes, see the Upgrade Guide.\\n\\n[characters 727-759]\\nWhat’s new in React 19 \\nActions \\n\\n[characters 761-1095]\\nA common use case in React apps is to perform a data mutation and then update state in response. For example, when a user submits a form to change their name, you will make an API request, and then handle the response. In the past, you would need to handle pending states, errors, optimistic updates, and sequential requests manually.\\n\\n[characters 1097-1167]\\nFor example, you could handle the pending and error state in useState:\\n\\n[characters 1169-1186]\\n// Before Actions\\n\\n[characters 1188-1213]\\nfunction UpdateName({}) {\\n\\n[characters 1215-1254]\\n  const [name, setName] = useState(\\\"\\\");\\n\\n[characters 1256-1299]\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n[characters 1301-1353]\\n  const [isPending, setIsPending] = useState(false);\\n\\n[characters 1357-1393]\\n  const handleSubmit = async () => {\\n\\n[characters 1420-1461]\\n    const error = await updateName(name);\\n\\n[characters 1463-1487]\\n    setIsPending(false);\\n\\n[characters 1553-1575]\\n    redirect(\\\"/path\\\");\\n\\n[characters 1608-1686]\\n      <input value={name} onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)} />\\n\\n[characters 1688-1746]\\n      <button onClick={handleSubmit} disabled={isPending}>\\n\\n[characters 1748-1762]\\n        Update\\n\\n[characters 1835-1988]\\nIn React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\\n\\n[characters 1990-2065]\\nFor example, you can use useTransition to handle the pending state for you:\\n\\n[characters 2067-2102]\\n// Using pending state from Actions\\n\\n[characters 2104-2129]\\nfunction UpdateName({}) {\\n\\n[characters 2131-2170]\\n  const [name, setName] = useState(\\\"\\\");\\n\\n[characters 2172-2215]\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n[characters 2217-2272]\\n  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();\\n\\n[characters 2276-2306]\\n  const handleSubmit = () => {\\n\\n[characters 2308-2341]\\n    startTransition(async () => {\\n\\n[characters 2343-2386]\\n      const error = await updateName(name);\\n\\n[characters 2460-2484]\\n      redirect(\\\"/path\\\");\\n\\n[characters 2525-2603]\\n      <input value={name} onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)} />\\n\\n[characters 2605-2663]\\n      <button onClick={handleSubmit} disabled={isPending}>\\n\\n[characters 2665-2679]\\n        Update\\n\\n[characters 2752-2996]\\nThe async transition will immediately set the isPending state to true, make the async request(s), and switch isPending to false after any transitions. This allows you to keep the current UI responsive and interactive while the data is changing.\\n\\n[characters 2998-3077]\\nNote\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \\n\\n[characters 3079-3132]\\nActions automatically manage submitting data for you:\\n\\n[characters 3134-3801]\\nPending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\\n\\n[characters 3803-4105]\\nBuilding on top of Actions, React 19 introduces useOptimistic to manage optimistic updates, and a new hook React.useActionState to handle common cases for Actions. In react-dom we’re adding <form> Actions to manage forms automatically and useFormStatus to support the common cases for Actions in forms.\\n\\n[characters 4107-4159]\\nIn React 19, the above example can be simplified to:\\n\\n[characters 4161-4203]\\n// Using <form> Actions and useActionState\\n\\n[characters 4205-4245]\\nfunction ChangeName({ name, setName }) {\\n\\n[characters 4247-4305]\\n  const [error, submitAction, isPending] = useActionState(\\n\\n[characters 4307-4347]\\n    async (previousState, formData) => {\\n\\n[characters 4349-4408]\\n      const error = await updateName(formData.get(\\\"name\\\"));\\n\\n[characters 4462-4486]\\n      redirect(\\\"/path\\\");\\n\\n[characters 4547-4579]\\n    <form action={submitAction}>\\n\\n[characters 4581-4620]\\n      <input type=\\\"text\\\" name=\\\"name\\\" />\\n\\n[characters 4622-4686]\\n      <button type=\\\"submit\\\" disabled={isPending}>Update</button>\\n\\n[characters 4721-4725]\\n    \",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"ref as a prop forwardRef class components cleanup functions for refs\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":0,\"charEnd\":24,\"text\":\"BLOG\\nCopy page\\nReact v19\"},{\"charStart\":26,\"charEnd\":61,\"text\":\"December 05, 2024 by The React Team\"},{\"charStart\":63,\"charEnd\":92,\"text\":\"Note\\nReact 19 is now stable! \"},{\"charStart\":94,\"charEnd\":172,\"text\":\"Additions since this post was originally shared with the React 19 RC in April:\"},{\"charStart\":174,\"charEnd\":290,\"text\":\"Pre-warming for suspended trees: see Improvements to Suspense.\\nReact DOM static APIs: see New React DOM Static APIs.\"},{\"charStart\":292,\"charEnd\":367,\"text\":\"The date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\"},{\"charStart\":369,\"charEnd\":403,\"text\":\"React v19 is now available on npm!\"},{\"charStart\":405,\"charEnd\":605,\"text\":\"In our React 19 Upgrade Guide, we shared step-by-step instructions for upgrading your app to React 19. In this post, we’ll give an overview of the new features in React 19, and how you can adopt them.\"},{\"charStart\":607,\"charEnd\":669,\"text\":\"What’s new in React 19\\nImprovements in React 19\\nHow to upgrade\"},{\"charStart\":671,\"charEnd\":725,\"text\":\"For a list of breaking changes, see the Upgrade Guide.\"},{\"charStart\":727,\"charEnd\":759,\"text\":\"What’s new in React 19 \\nActions \"},{\"charStart\":761,\"charEnd\":1095,\"text\":\"A common use case in React apps is to perform a data mutation and then update state in response. For example, when a user submits a form to change their name, you will make an API request, and then handle the response. In the past, you would need to handle pending states, errors, optimistic updates, and sequential requests manually.\"},{\"charStart\":1097,\"charEnd\":1167,\"text\":\"For example, you could handle the pending and error state in useState:\"},{\"charStart\":1169,\"charEnd\":1186,\"text\":\"// Before Actions\"},{\"charStart\":1188,\"charEnd\":1213,\"text\":\"function UpdateName({}) {\"},{\"charStart\":1215,\"charEnd\":1254,\"text\":\"  const [name, setName] = useState(\\\"\\\");\"},{\"charStart\":1256,\"charEnd\":1299,\"text\":\"  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\"},{\"charStart\":1301,\"charEnd\":1353,\"text\":\"  const [isPending, setIsPending] = useState(false);\"},{\"charStart\":1357,\"charEnd\":1393,\"text\":\"  const handleSubmit = async () => {\"},{\"charStart\":1420,\"charEnd\":1461,\"text\":\"    const error = await updateName(name);\"},{\"charStart\":1463,\"charEnd\":1487,\"text\":\"    setIsPending(false);\"},{\"charStart\":1553,\"charEnd\":1575,\"text\":\"    redirect(\\\"/path\\\");\"},{\"charStart\":1608,\"charEnd\":1686,\"text\":\"      <input value={name} onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)} />\"},{\"charStart\":1688,\"charEnd\":1746,\"text\":\"      <button onClick={handleSubmit} disabled={isPending}>\"},{\"charStart\":1748,\"charEnd\":1762,\"text\":\"        Update\"},{\"charStart\":1835,\"charEnd\":1988,\"text\":\"In React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\"},{\"charStart\":1990,\"charEnd\":2065,\"text\":\"For example, you can use useTransition to handle the pending state for you:\"},{\"charStart\":2067,\"charEnd\":2102,\"text\":\"// Using pending state from Actions\"},{\"charStart\":2104,\"charEnd\":2129,\"text\":\"function UpdateName({}) {\"},{\"charStart\":2131,\"charEnd\":2170,\"text\":\"  const [name, setName] = useState(\\\"\\\");\"},{\"charStart\":2172,\"charEnd\":2215,\"text\":\"  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\"},{\"charStart\":2217,\"charEnd\":2272,\"text\":\"  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();\"},{\"charStart\":2276,\"charEnd\":2306,\"text\":\"  const handleSubmit = () => {\"},{\"charStart\":2308,\"charEnd\":2341,\"text\":\"    startTransition(async () => {\"},{\"charStart\":2343,\"charEnd\":2386,\"text\":\"      const error = await updateName(name);\"},{\"charStart\":2460,\"charEnd\":2484,\"text\":\"      redirect(\\\"/path\\\");\"},{\"charStart\":2525,\"charEnd\":2603,\"text\":\"      <input value={name} onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)} />\"},{\"charStart\":2605,\"charEnd\":2663,\"text\":\"      <button onClick={handleSubmit} disabled={isPending}>\"},{\"charStart\":2665,\"charEnd\":2679,\"text\":\"        Update\"},{\"charStart\":2752,\"charEnd\":2996,\"text\":\"The async transition will immediately set the isPending state to true, make the async request(s), and switch isPending to false after any transitions. This allows you to keep the current UI responsive and interactive while the data is changing.\"},{\"charStart\":2998,\"charEnd\":3077,\"text\":\"Note\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \"},{\"charStart\":3079,\"charEnd\":3132,\"text\":\"Actions automatically manage submitting data for you:\"},{\"charStart\":3134,\"charEnd\":3801,\"text\":\"Pending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\"},{\"charStart\":3803,\"charEnd\":4105,\"text\":\"Building on top of Actions, React 19 introduces useOptimistic to manage optimistic updates, and a new hook React.useActionState to handle common cases for Actions. In react-dom we’re adding <form> Actions to manage forms automatically and useFormStatus to support the common cases for Actions in forms.\"},{\"charStart\":4107,\"charEnd\":4159,\"text\":\"In React 19, the above example can be simplified to:\"},{\"charStart\":4161,\"charEnd\":4203,\"text\":\"// Using <form> Actions and useActionState\"},{\"charStart\":4205,\"charEnd\":4245,\"text\":\"function ChangeName({ name, setName }) {\"},{\"charStart\":4247,\"charEnd\":4305,\"text\":\"  const [error, submitAction, isPending] = useActionState(\"},{\"charStart\":4307,\"charEnd\":4347,\"text\":\"    async (previousState, formData) => {\"},{\"charStart\":4349,\"charEnd\":4408,\"text\":\"      const error = await updateName(formData.get(\\\"name\\\"));\"},{\"charStart\":4462,\"charEnd\":4486,\"text\":\"      redirect(\\\"/path\\\");\"},{\"charStart\":4547,\"charEnd\":4579,\"text\":\"    <form action={submitAction}>\"},{\"charStart\":4581,\"charEnd\":4620,\"text\":\"      <input type=\\\"text\\\" name=\\\"name\\\" />\"},{\"charStart\":4622,\"charEnd\":4686,\"text\":\"      <button type=\\\"submit\\\" disabled={isPending}>Update</button>\"},{\"charStart\":4721,\"charEnd\":4725,\"text\":\"    \"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-d4639fa5-93d3-4c19-b0c5-3adfc5860d9e\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\aece5e58c4cf64f78c9e3bccc03bda00a1c56ee594e7081cc97810825cad01da\\\\session-3a0d1e90-b75c-49be-8062-fba6e990a659\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca.txt\"}"
