          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca\",\"url\":\"https://react.dev/blog/2024/12/05/react-19\",\"title\":\"React v19 – React\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:19.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 292-367]\\nThe date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\\n\\n[characters 1188-1213]\\nfunction UpdateName({}) {\\n\\n[characters 1835-1988]\\nIn React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\\n\\n[characters 2104-2129]\\nfunction UpdateName({}) {\\n\\n[characters 2998-3077]\\nNote\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \\n\\n[characters 3134-3801]\\nPending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\\n\\n[characters 4205-4245]\\nfunction ChangeName({ name, setName }) {\\n\\n[characters 5290-5562]\\nuseActionState accepts a function (the “Action”), and returns a wrapped Action to call. This works because Actions compose. When the wrapped Action is called, useActionState will return the last result of the Action as data, and the pending state of the Action as pending.\\n\\n[characters 5821-6062]\\nActions are also integrated with React 19’s new <form> features for react-dom. We’ve added support for passing functions as the action and formAction props of <form>, <input>, and <button> elements to automatically submit forms with Actions:\\n\\n[characters 6064-6094]\\n<form action={actionFunction}>\\n\\n[characters 6096-6290]\\nWhen a <form> Action succeeds, React will automatically reset the form for uncontrolled components. If you need to reset the <form> manually, you can call the new requestFormReset React DOM API.\\n\\n[characters 6410-6678]\\nIn design systems, it’s common to write design components that need access to information about the <form> they’re in, without drilling props down to the component. This can be done via Context, but to make the common case easier, we’ve added a new hook useFormStatus:\\n\\n[characters 6724-6749]\\nfunction DesignButton() {\\n\\n[characters 7246-7296]\\nfunction ChangeName({currentName, onUpdateName}) {\\n\\n[characters 8371-8409]\\nfunction Comments({commentsPromise}) {\\n\\n[characters 8581-8615]\\nfunction Page({commentsPromise}) {\\n\\n[characters 9541-9571]\\nfunction Heading({children}) {\\n\\n[characters 10542-10575]\\nasync function handler(request) {\\n\\n[characters 11088-11131]\\nReact Server Components \\nServer Components \\n\\n[characters 11133-11493]\\nServer Components are a new option that allows rendering components ahead of time, before bundling, in an environment separate from your client application or SSR server. This separate environment is the “server” in React Server Components. Server Components can run once at build time on your CI server, or they can be run for each request using a web server.\\n\\n[characters 11495-11796]\\nReact 19 includes all of the React Server Components features included from the Canary channel. This means libraries that ship with Server Components can now target React 19 as a peer dependency with a react-server export condition for use in frameworks that support the Full-stack React Architecture.\\n\\n[characters 11798-11849]\\nNote\\nHow do I build support for Server Components? \\n\\n[characters 11851-12096]\\nWhile React Server Components in React 19 are stable and will not break between minor versions, the underlying APIs used to implement a React Server Components bundler or framework do not follow semver and may break be\n\n[... tool result middle pruned ...]\n\n\":\"For more, see the docs for React Server Components.\"},{\"charStart\":12443,\"charEnd\":12529,\"text\":\"Server Actions allow Client Components to call async functions executed on the server.\"},{\"charStart\":12531,\"charEnd\":12853,\"text\":\"When a Server Action is defined with the \\\"use server\\\" directive, your framework will automatically create a reference to the server function, and pass that reference to the Client Component. When that function is called on the client, React will send a request to the server to execute the function, and return the result.\"},{\"charStart\":12855,\"charEnd\":12905,\"text\":\"Note\\nThere is no directive for Server Components. \"},{\"charStart\":12907,\"charEnd\":12942,\"text\":\"A common misunderstanding is that S\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-d4639fa5-93d3-4c19-b0c5-3adfc5860d9e\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\aece5e58c4cf64f78c9e3bccc03bda00a1c56ee594e7081cc97810825cad01da\\\\session-3a0d1e90-b75c-49be-8062-fba6e990a659\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca.txt\"}"
