          "type": "text",
          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca\",\"url\":\"https://react.dev/blog/2024/12/05/react-19\",\"title\":\"React v19 – React\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:19.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 292-367]\\nThe date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\\n\\n[characters 1188-1213]\\nfunction UpdateName({}) {\\n\\n[characters 1835-1988]\\nIn React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\\n\\n[characters 2104-2129]\\nfunction UpdateName({}) {\\n\\n[characters 2998-3077]\\nNote\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \\n\\n[characters 3134-3801]\\nPending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\\n\\n[characters 4205-4245]\\nfunction ChangeName({ name, setName }) {\\n\\n[characters 5290-5562]\\nuseActionState accepts a function (the “Action”), and returns a wrapped Action to call. This works because Actions compose. When the wrapped Action is called, useActionState will return the last result of the Action as data, and the pending state of the Action as pending.\\n\\n[characters 5821-6062]\\nActions are also integrated with React 19’s new <form> features for react-dom. We’ve added support for passing functions as the action and formAction props of <form>, <input>, and <button> elements to automatically submit forms with Actions:\\n\\n[characters 6064-6094]\\n<form action={actionFunction}>\\n\\n[characters 6724-6749]\\nfunction DesignButton() {\\n\\n[characters 7246-7296]\\nfunction ChangeName({currentName, onUpdateName}) {\\n\\n[characters 8371-8409]\\nfunction Comments({commentsPromise}) {\\n\\n[characters 8581-8615]\\nfunction Page({commentsPromise}) {\\n\\n[characters 9541-9571]\\nfunction Heading({children}) {\\n\\n[characters 10542-10575]\\nasync function handler(request) {\\n\\n[characters 12443-12529]\\nServer Actions allow Client Components to call async functions executed on the server.\\n\\n[characters 12531-12853]\\nWhen a Server Action is defined with the \\\"use server\\\" directive, your framework will automatically create a reference to the server function, and pass that reference to the Client Component. When that function is called on the client, React will send a request to the server to execute the function, and return the result.\\n\\n[characters 13337-13377]\\nImprovements in React 19 \\nref as a prop \\n\\n[characters 13379-13458]\\nStarting in React 19, you can now access ref as a prop for function components:\\n\\n[characters 13460-13498]\\nfunction MyInput({placeholder, ref}) {\\n\\n[characters 13500-13554]\\n  return <input placeholder={placeholder} ref={ref} />\\n\\n[characters 13568-13589]\\n<MyInput ref={ref} />\\n\\n[characters 13591-13807]\\nNew function components will no longer need forwardRef, and we will be publishing a codemod to automatically update your components to use the new ref prop. In future versions we will deprecate and remove forwardRef.\\n\\n[characters 13815-13906]\\nrefs passed to classes are not passed as props since they reference the component instance.\\n\\n[characters 15585-15611]\\nfunction App({children}) {\\n\\n[characters 15871-15898]\\nCleanup functions for refs \\n\\n[characters 15900-15963]\\nWe now support returning a cleanup function from ref callbacks:\\n\\n[characters 15973-15990]\\n  ref={(ref) => {\\n\\n[characters 15992-16010]\\n    // ref created\\n\\n[characters 16014-16060]\\n    // NEW: return a cleanup function to reset\\n\\n[characters 16062-16110]\\n    // the ref when element is removed from DOM.\\n\\n[characters 16132-16152]\\n      // ref cleanup\\n\\n[characters 16172-16345]\\nWhen the component unmounts, React will call the cleanup function returned from the ref callback. This works for DOM refs, refs to class components, and useImperativeHandle.\\n\\n[characters 16353-16507]\\nPreviously, React would call ref functions with null when unmounting the component. If your ref returns a cleanup function, React will now skip this step.\\n\\n[characters 16509-16597]\\nIn future versions, we will deprecate calling refs with null when unmounting components.\\n\\n[characters 16599-16792]\\nDue to the introduction of ref cleanup functions, returning anything else from a ref callback will now be rejected by TypeScript. The fix is usually to stop using implicit returns, for example:\\n\\n[characters 16794-16841]\\n- <div ref={current => (instance = current)} />\\n\\n[characters 16843-16890]\\n+ <div ref={current => {instance = current}} />\\n\\n[characters 16892-17080]\\nThe original code returned the instance of the HTMLDivElement and TypeScript wouldn’t know if this was supposed to be a cleanup function or if you didn’t want to return a cleanup function.\\n\\n[characters 17082-17148]\\nYou can codemod this pattern with no-implicit-ref-callback-return.\\n\\n[characters 17240-17274]\\nfunction Search({deferredValue}) {\\n\\n[characters 17741-17818]\\nIn HTML, document metadata tags like <title>, <link>, and <meta> are reserved\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"ref cleanup function ref callback cleanup\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":292,\"charEnd\":367,\"text\":\"The date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\"},{\"charStart\":1188,\"charEnd\":1213,\"text\":\"function UpdateName({}) {\"},{\"charStart\":1835,\"charEnd\":1988,\"text\":\"In React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\"},{\"charStart\":2104,\"charEnd\":2129,\"text\":\"function UpdateName({}) {\"},{\"charStart\":2998,\"charEnd\":3077,\"text\":\"Note\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \"},{\"charStart\":3134,\"charEnd\":3801,\"text\":\"Pending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\"},{\"charStart\":4205,\"charEnd\":4245,\"text\":\"function ChangeName({ name, setName }) {\"},{\"charStart\":5290,\"charEnd\":5562,\"text\":\"useActionState accepts a function (the “Action”), and returns a wrapped Action to call. This works because Actions compose. When the wrapped Action is called, useActionState will return the last result of the Action as data, and the pending state of the Action as pending.\"},{\"charStart\":5821,\"charEnd\":6062,\"text\":\"Actions are also integrated with React 19’s new <form> features for react-dom. We’ve added support for passing functions as the action and formAction props of <form>, <input>, and <button> elements to automatically submit forms with Actions:\"},{\"charStart\":6064,\"charEnd\":6094,\"text\":\"<form action={actionFunction}>\"},{\"charStart\":6724,\"charEnd\":6749,\"text\":\"function DesignButton() {\"},{\"charStart\":7246,\"charEnd\":7296,\"text\":\"function ChangeName({currentName, onUpdateName}) {\"},{\"charStart\":8371,\"charEnd\":8409,\"text\":\"function Comments({commentsPromise}) {\"},{\"charStart\":8581,\"charEnd\":8615,\"text\":\"function Page({commentsPromise}) {\"},{\"charStart\":9541,\"charEnd\":9571,\"text\":\"function Heading({children}) {\"},{\"charStart\":10542,\"charEnd\":10575,\"text\":\"async function handler(request) {\"},{\"charStart\":12443,\"charEnd\":12529,\"text\":\"Server Actions allow Client Components to call async functions executed on the server.\"},{\"charStart\":12531,\"charEnd\":12853,\"text\":\"When a Server Action is defined with the \\\"use server\\\" directive, your framework will automatically create a reference to the server function, and pass that reference to the Client Component. When that function is called on the client, React will send a request to the server to execute the function, and return the result.\"},{\"charStart\":13337,\"charEnd\":13377,\"text\":\"Improvements in React 19 \\nref as a prop \"},{\"charStart\":13379,\"charEnd\":13458,\"text\":\"Starting in React 19, you can now access ref as a prop for function components:\"},{\"charStart\":13460,\"charEnd\":13498,\"text\":\"function MyInput({placeholder, ref}) {\"},{\"charStart\":13500,\"charEnd\":13554,\"text\":\"  return <input placeholder={placeholder} ref={ref} />\"},{\"charStart\":13568,\"charEnd\":13589,\"text\":\"<MyInput ref={ref} />\"},{\"charStart\":13591,\"charEnd\":13807,\"text\":\"New function components will no longer need forwardRef, and we will be publishing a codemod to automatically update your components to use the new ref prop. In future versions we will deprecate and remove forwardRef.\"},{\"charStart\":13815,\"charEnd\":13906,\"text\":\"refs passed to classes are not passed as props since they reference the component instance.\"},{\"charStart\":15585,\"charEnd\":15611,\"text\":\"function App({children}) {\"},{\"charStart\":15871,\"charEnd\":15898,\"text\":\"Cleanup functions for refs \"},{\"charStart\":15900,\"charEnd\":15963,\"text\":\"We now support returning a cleanup function from ref callbacks:\"},{\"charStart\":15973,\"charEnd\":15990,\"text\":\"  ref={(ref) => {\"},{\"charStart\":15992,\"charEnd\":16010,\"text\":\"    // ref created\"},{\"charStart\":16014,\"charEnd\":16060,\"text\":\"    // NEW: return a cleanup function to reset\"},{\"charStart\":16062,\"charEnd\":16110,\"text\":\"    // the ref when element is removed from DOM.\"},{\"charStart\":16132,\"charEnd\":16152,\"text\":\"      // ref cleanup\"},{\"charStart\":16172,\"charEnd\":16345,\"text\":\"When the component unmounts, React will call the cleanup function returned from the ref callback. This works for DOM refs, refs to class components, and useImperativeHandle.\"},{\"charStart\":16353,\"charEnd\":16507,\"text\":\"Previously, React would call ref functions with null when unmounting the component. If your ref returns a cleanup function, React will now skip this step.\"},{\"charStart\":16509,\"charEnd\":16597,\"text\":\"In future versions, we will deprecate calling refs with null when unmounting components.\"},{\"charStart\":16599,\"charEnd\":16792,\"text\":\"Due to the introduction of ref cleanup functions, returning anything else from a ref callback will now be rejected by TypeScript. The fix is usually to stop using implicit returns, for example:\"},{\"charStart\":16794,\"charEnd\":16841,\"text\":\"- <div ref={current => (instance = current)} />\"},{\"charStart\":16843,\"charEnd\":16890,\"text\":\"+ <div ref={current => {instance = current}} />\"},{\"charStart\":16892,\"charEnd\":17080,\"text\":\"The original code returned the instance of the HTMLDivElement and TypeScript wouldn’t know if this was supposed to be a cleanup function or if you didn’t want to return a cleanup function.\"},{\"charStart\":17082,\"charEnd\":17148,\"text\":\"You can codemod this pattern with no-implicit-ref-callback-return.\"},{\"charStart\":17240,\"charEnd\":17274,\"text\":\"function Search({deferredValue}) {\"},{\"charStart\":17741,\"charEnd\":17818,\"text\":\"In HTML, document metadata tags like <title>, <link>, and <meta> are reserved\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-d4639fa5-93d3-4c19-b0c5-3adfc5860d9e\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\aece5e58c4cf64f78c9e3bccc03bda00a1c56ee594e7081cc97810825cad01da\\\\session-3a0d1e90-b75c-49be-8062-fba6e990a659\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca.txt\"}"
