          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-72004579-59ba-4796-a7d7-673c17cfc8ca\",\"url\":\"https://react.dev/blog/2024/12/05/react-19\",\"title\":\"React v19 – React\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T03:08:19.935Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 174-290]\\nPre-warming for suspended trees: see Improvements to Suspense.\\nReact DOM static APIs: see New React DOM Static APIs.\\n\\n[characters 292-367]\\nThe date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\\n\\n[characters 405-605]\\nIn our React 19 Upgrade Guide, we shared step-by-step instructions for upgrading your app to React 19. In this post, we’ll give an overview of the new features in React 19, and how you can adopt them.\\n\\n[characters 671-725]\\nFor a list of breaking changes, see the Upgrade Guide.\\n\\n[characters 761-1095]\\nA common use case in React apps is to perform a data mutation and then update state in response. For example, when a user submits a form to change their name, you will make an API request, and then handle the response. In the past, you would need to handle pending states, errors, optimistic updates, and sequential requests manually.\\n\\n[characters 1097-1167]\\nFor example, you could handle the pending and error state in useState:\\n\\n[characters 1169-1186]\\n// Before Actions\\n\\n[characters 1531-1544]\\n      return;\\n\\n[characters 1585-1595]\\n  return (\\n\\n[characters 1835-1988]\\nIn React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\\n\\n[characters 1990-2065]\\nFor example, you can use useTransition to handle the pending state for you:\\n\\n[characters 2434-2449]\\n        return;\\n\\n[characters 2502-2512]\\n  return (\\n\\n[characters 2998-3077]\\nNote\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \\n\\n[characters 3079-3132]\\nActions automatically manage submitting data for you:\\n\\n[characters 3134-3801]\\nPending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\\n\\n[characters 3803-4105]\\nBuilding on top of Actions, React 19 introduces useOptimistic to manage optimistic updates, and a new hook React.useActionState to handle common cases for Actions. In react-dom we’re adding <form> Actions to manage forms automatically and useFormStatus to support the common cases for Actions in forms.\\n\\n[characters 4161-4203]\\n// Using <form> Actions and useActionState\\n\\n[characters 4307-4347]\\n    async (previousState, formData) => {\\n\\n[characters 4349-4408]\\n      const error = await updateName(formData.get(\\\"name\\\"));\\n\\n[characters 4430-4451]\\n        return error;\\n\\n[characters 4488-4506]\\n      return null;\\n\\n[characters 4535-4545]\\n  return (\\n\\n[characters 4547-4579]\\n    <form action={submitAction}>\\n\\n[characters 4721-4732]\\n    </form>\\n\\n[characters 4854-4944]\\nTo make the common cases easier for Actions, we’ve added a new hook called useActionState:\\n\\n[characters 5107-5156]\\n      // You can return any result of the action.\\n\\n[characters 5158-5198]\\n      // Here, we return only the error.\\n\\n[characters 5200-5219]\\n      return error;\\n\\n[characters 5253-5269]\\n    return null;\\n\\n[characters 5290-5562]\\nuseActionState accepts a function (the “Action”), and returns a wrapped Action to call. This works because Actions compose. When the wrapped Action is called, useActionState will return the last result of the Action as data, and the pending state of the Action as pending.\\n\\n[characters 5570-5708]\\nReact.useActionState was previously called ReactDOM.useFormState in the Canary releases, but we’ve renamed it and deprecated useFormState.\\n\\n[characters 5710-5735]\\nSee #28491 for more info.\\n\\n[characters 5737-5791]\\nFor more information, see the docs for useActionState.\\n\\n[characters 5793-5819]\\nReact DOM: <form> Actions \\n\\n[characters 5821-6062]\\nActions are also integrated with React 19’s new <form> features for react-dom. We’ve added support for passing functions as the action and formAction props of <form>, <input>, and <button> elements to automatically submit forms with Actions:\\n\\n[characters 6064-6094]\\n<form action={actionFunction}>\\n\\n[characters 6096-6290]\\nWhen a <form> Action succeeds, React will automatically reset the form for uncontrolled components. If you need to reset the <form> manually, you can call the new requestFormReset React DOM API.\\n\\n[characters 6292-6371]\\nFor more information, see the react-dom docs for <form>, <input>, and <button>.\\n\\n[characters 6373-6408]\\nReact DOM: New hook: useFormStatus \\n\\n[characters 6410-6678]\\nIn design systems, it’s common to write design components that need access to information about the <form> they’re in, without drilling props down to the component. This can be done via Context, but to make the common case easier, we’ve added a new hook useFormStatus:\\n\\n[characters 6680-6720]\\nimport {useFormStatus} from 'react-dom';\\n\\n[characters 6751-6755]\\n  co\",\"truncated\":true,\"query\":\"Cleanup functions for refs ref callbacks TypeScript rejected return\",\"excerpts\":[{\"charStart\":174,\"charEnd\":290,\"text\":\"Pre-warming for suspended trees: see Improvements to Suspense.\\nReact DOM static APIs: see New React DOM Static APIs.\"},{\"charStart\":292,\"charEnd\":367,\"text\":\"The date for this post has been updated to reflect the stable release date.\"},{\"charStart\":405,\"charEnd\":605,\"text\":\"In our React 19 Upgrade Guide, we shared step-by-step instructions for upgrading your app to React 19. In this post, we’ll give an overview of the new features in React 19, and how you can adopt them.\"},{\"charStart\":671,\"charEnd\":725,\"text\":\"For a list of breaking changes, see the Upgrade Guide.\"},{\"charStart\":761,\"charEnd\":1095,\"text\":\"A common use case in React apps is to perform a data mutation and then update state in response. For example, when a user submits a form to change their name, you will make an API request, and then handle the response. In the past, you would need to handle pending states, errors, optimistic updates, and sequential requests manually.\"},{\"charStart\":1097,\"charEnd\":1167,\"text\":\"For example, you could handle the pending and error state in useState:\"},{\"charStart\":1169,\"charEnd\":1186,\"text\":\"// Before Actions\"},{\"charStart\":1531,\"charEnd\":1544,\"text\":\"      return;\"},{\"charStart\":1585,\"charEnd\":1595,\"text\":\"  return (\"},{\"charStart\":1835,\"charEnd\":1988,\"text\":\"In React 19, we’re adding support for using async functions in transitions to handle pending states, errors, forms, and optimistic updates automatically.\"},{\"charStart\":1990,\"charEnd\":2065,\"text\":\"For example, you can use useTransition to handle the pending state for you:\"},{\"charStart\":2434,\"charEnd\":2449,\"text\":\"        return;\"},{\"charStart\":2502,\"charEnd\":2512,\"text\":\"  return (\"},{\"charStart\":2998,\"charEnd\":3077,\"text\":\"Note\\nBy convention, functions that use async transitions are called “Actions”. \"},{\"charStart\":3079,\"charEnd\":3132,\"text\":\"Actions automatically manage submitting data for you:\"},{\"charStart\":3134,\"charEnd\":3801,\"text\":\"Pending state: Actions provide a pending state that starts at the beginning of a request and automatically resets when the final state update is committed.\\nOptimistic updates: Actions support the new useOptimistic hook so you can show users instant feedback while the requests are submitting.\\nError handling: Actions provide error handling so you can display Error Boundaries when a request fails, and revert optimistic updates to their original value automatically.\\nForms: <form> elements now support passing functions to the action and formAction props. Passing functions to the action props use Actions by default and reset the form automatically after submission.\"},{\"charStart\":3803,\"charEnd\":4105,\"text\":\"Building on top of Actions, React 19 introduces useOptimistic to manage optimistic updates, and a new hook React.useActionState to handle common cases for Actions. In react-dom we’re adding <form> Actions to manage forms automatically and useFormStatus to support the common cases for Actions in forms.\"},{\"charStart\":4161,\"charEnd\":4203,\"text\":\"// Using <form> Actions and useActionState\"},{\"charStart\":4307,\"charEnd\":4347,\"text\":\"    async (previousState, formData) => {\"},{\"charStart\":4349,\"charEnd\":4408,\"text\":\"      const error = await updateName(formData.get(\\\"name\\\"));\"},{\"charStart\":4430,\"charEnd\":4451,\"text\":\"        return error;\"},{\"charStart\":4488,\"charEnd\":4506,\"text\":\"      return null;\"},{\"charStart\":4535,\"charEnd\":4545,\"text\":\"  return (\"},{\"charStart\":4547,\"charEnd\":4579,\"text\":\"    <form action={submitAction}>\"},{\"charStart\":4721,\"charEnd\":4732,\"text\":\"    </form>\"},{\"charStart\":4854,\"charEnd\":4944,\"text\":\"To make the common cases easier for Actions, we’ve added a new hook called useActionState:\"},{\"charStart\":5107,\"charEnd\":5156,\"text\":\"      // You can return any result of the action.\"},{\"charStart\":5158,\"charEnd\":5198,\"text\":\"      // Here, we return only the error.\"},{\"charStart\":5200,\"charEnd\":5219,\"text\":\"      return error;\"},{\"charStart\":5253,\"charEnd\":5269,\"text\":\"    return null;\"},{\"charStart\":5290,\"charEnd\":5562,\"text\":\"useActionState accepts a function (the “Action”), and returns a wrapped Action to call. This works because Actions compose. When the wrapped Action is called, useActionState will return the last result of the Action as data, and the pending state of the Action as pending.\"},{\"charStart\":5570,\"charEnd\":5708,\"text\":\"React.useActionState was previously called ReactDOM.useFormState in the Canary releases, but we’ve renamed it and deprecated useFormState.\"},{\"charStart\":5710,\"charEnd\":5735,\"text\":\"See #28491 for more info.\"},{\"charStart\":5737,\"charEnd\":5791,\"text\":\"For more information, see the docs for useActionState.\"},{\"charStart\":5793,\"charEnd\":5819,\"text\":\"React DOM: <form> Actions \"},{\"charStart\":5821,\"charEnd\":6062,\"text\":\"Actions are also integrated with React 19’s new <form> features for react-dom. We’ve added support for passing functions as the action and formAction props of <form>, <input>, and <button> elements to automatically submit forms with Actions:\"},{\"charStart\":6064,\"charEnd\":6094,\"text\":\"<form action={actionFunction}>\"},{\"charStart\":6096,\"charEnd\":6290,\"text\":\"When a <form> Action succeeds, React will automatically reset the form for uncontrolled components. If you need to reset the <form> manually, you can call the new requestFormReset React DOM API.\"},{\"charStart\":6292,\"charEnd\":6371,\"text\":\"For more information, see the react-dom docs for <form>, <input>, and <button>.\"},{\"charStart\":6373,\"charEnd\":6408,\"text\":\"React DOM: New hook: useFormStatus \"},{\"charStart\":6410,\"charEnd\":6678,\"text\":\"In design systems, it’s common to write design components that need access to information about the <form> they’re in, without drilling props down to the component. This can be done via Context, but to make the common case easier, we’ve added a new hook useFormStatus:\"},{\"charStart\":6680,\"charEnd\":672