n to progress independently while referring to the same core semantics.\"},{\"charStart\":15663,\"charEnd\":16205,\"text\":\"Each message is either a request or a response. A client constructs request messages that communicate its intentions and routes those messages toward an identified origin server. A server listens for requests, parses each message received, interprets the message semantics in relation to the identified target resource, and responds to that request with one or more response messages. The client examines received responses to see if its intentions were carried out, determining what to do next based on the status codes and content received.\"},{\"charStart\":16207,\"charEnd\":16507,\"text\":\"HTTP semantics include the intentions defined by each request method (Section 9), extensions to those semantics that might be described in request header fields, status codes that describe the response (Section 15), and other control data and resource metadata that might be given in response fields.\"},{\"charStart\":16509,\"charEnd\":16745,\"text\":\"Semantics also include representation metadata that describe how content is intended to be interpreted by a recipient, request header fields that might influence content selection, and the various selection algorithms that are collectiv\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-d4639fa5-93d3-4c19-b0c5-3adfc5860d9e\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\aece5e58c4cf64f78c9e3bccc03bda00a1c56ee594e7081cc97810825cad01da\\\\session-3891b7ef-66b8-4a25-a922-5f7f56b0f910\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-cec51373-9a98-408a-86f8-b1347648c75e.txt\"}"
